Let me now systematically decide each proposal. I need to ground in Vision/use cases and my Sun Tzu items.

Let me recall my Sun Tzu list items (the numbered list 1-20). Key items I might cite:

1. Reckon before battle (five matters, seven comparisons; ground→measure→quantity→number→weighing→victory; more calculation wins, less loses). This is about laying out the full ground and counting clauses before committing. Good for "count every clause, name it, don't leave one unchecked" and "the instrument must measure the thing".

2. Make yourself unbeatable first; win then seek battle. "Do not rely on their not coming; rely on having the means." Good for "don't rely on the agent behaving; build the tool's own guarantee." Also "you can make yourself unbeatable, not the enemy."

3. Whole over broken; strike plans first, cities last. Whole > broken; the best is to keep whole. Could apply to "keep the requirement whole, not split". Hmm, that's a nice application: preferring the whole to the broken — a requirement kept whole. Also "a hundred victories in a hundred battles is not the best of the best."

4. Know whether you can fight; know the other and yourself. Five things tell who wins; knowing self and other = no danger. Applies to "state what you can establish and what you can't."

5. Move only when it accords with advantage; never from anger. Do not move without advantage, do not fight unless in danger. "not worth it" — reject proposals that add no value.

6. In deliberating, mix harm into benefit. Mix harm into benefit so the undertaking can be carried out and trouble resolved. Good for "say what each proposal costs."

7. War is the way of deception. A false link is the deceit the check must catch. Also "attack where unprepared."

8. Value victory not duration; speed; supply from the enemy. Clumsy speed vs. prolonged skill. Good for "the cheap decisive text change now, not exhaustive rewording."

9. Bring the other side to you; do not be brought. Seize what they cherish; divert. "Set the terms." Good for "the requirement should fix the tool's obligation, not chase the agent's."

10. Avoid the solid, strike the empty; have no constant form. Make them show form while you show none; no fixed form. Good for "a wording that is formless/adaptable" or "excludes unrelated content that has no form"? Hmm. Also "everyone knows the form by which I won, no one the form by which I controlled." Could apply to builder-readable checks. But here it's wording.

11. Foreknowledge must come from people; pay for it. Knowledge must come from a named source, not from omens or arithmetic. Good for "a check's source must be named; 'known' has no owner" and "if the requirement needs a fact, get it from a named source." Also "do not grudge the payment" (pay for the foreknowledge). Strong candidate for proposal 2 ("known" → "the type checker can establish"): the owner of "known" must be named.

12. Seek it in 勢, made of regular and irregular. 奇正, combinations without end; seek victory in 勢 not in people. Good for "the wording should work through the structure (the rule), not rely on the person (the agent)" — i.e., don't demand correctness from the agent; set the conditions. Great fit for proposals 1/7 (don't put the agent's duty where a tool check lives; instead make the tool's condition unbeatable). Actually this item says: "the skilled fighter seeks victory in 勢 and does not demand it of his people, so he can choose his people and rely on 勢." That's a strong argument for: put the burden on the tool's deterministic output (勢 / conditions), not on the agent's good behavior (人 / people). So the tool half should be a real Fails-if condition; the agent half can remain but cannot be the tool's guarantee.

13. Not every road is taken, and not every command accepted. "途有所不由... 君命有所不受." There are roads not to be taken. Good for "reject a proposal; not every proposed clause is accepted." Also "the requirement does not accept every command laid on it" — a requirement shouldn't be forced to carry the evaluator's every check. Good for "a requirement is not split for the checker's convenience."

14. Choose the action by the ratio of forces. Ten to one surround, five attack, two divide... don't mass on the same spot. Good for "do not add a second clause for something already covered" (avoid duplicating). Also "more is not what is valued."

15. The general's faults: five dangers, six defeats. Virtues as levers; the check's own faults are the handle against it. Good for "a proposal's own weakness" and "an over-strict wording is a lever against correct answers."

16. Orders clear first, then enforced; care that cannot command is useless. "If orders are not clear, it is the general's fault; if clear and not followed, the officers' fault." Good for "the wording must be clear; a vague word is the general's fault." Also "spoiled children" — a requirement that cannot command is useless. Strong for "must not mislead" and for making wording enforceable.

17. Learn the enemy by probing and by signs. "策之而知得失之計" — reckon them to know the plan; learn by probing. Good for "test/observe; know by acting" and "the wording should be decidable by evidence." Also "signs" — read intent from what can be seen.

18. Spirit, heart, strength and change: meet theirs with yours. Avoid the keen, strike when it flags; with order await disorder. Good for timing: do the cheap thing now, the hard thing later; don't attack crack troops. Maybe "reject the hard/overreaching now."

19. Name the ground, act as prescribed. Identify the ground then act as prescribed. Troops with no way out fight. Good for "name the situation (the command) then prescribe the response" — a table from conditions to responses. Strong for A5: name the command, then the answer it owes. Also "the same chapter gives a second list for each ground of what the commander himself does" — tool vs agent.

20. Which house falls first: judge by the land unit, the tax, and what they breed. Judging by structure. Probably not needed.

